><div>I can still remember how different that gold cartridge felt from every other Nintendo game I owned. Before I even pressed Start, The Legend of Zelda already felt like something important.

As a kid, I would sit on the floor way too close to the TV and disappear into Hyrule for hours. Most of the time I had absolutely no idea where I was going. I burned random bushes, bombed every wall that looked suspicious and wandered around as Link hoping I would accidentally discover a hidden cave, a new item or another piece of the Triforce.

There were no easy answers. You learned secrets from a friend at school, scribbled notes on paper and raced home to see if the rumor was actually true. Every discovery felt like it belonged to you.

The Legend of Zelda was released in the US 39 years ago today, and I can still hear that opening music in my head. That original NES game did not just feel like something I played. It felt like my own adventure.

Who else remembers seeing that gold cartridge for the first time?</div></div>]]></description><guid>https://imgur.com/a/0Jpuy11</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[TheRainbowegoSweet007]]></dc:creator></item><item><title>Let them all come, we'll arrest the lot.</title><link>https://imgur.com/gallery/V1DzHRK</link><description><![CDATA[<div><img alt="" src="https://i.imgur.com/15sL6dI.jpg" style="max-width: 90%; height: auto;"></div><div><span></span></div><div>What I saw is he was arrested after aiming his vehicle at a crowd of people.</div></div>]]></description><guid>https://imgur.com/a/V1DzHRK</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Raventhief]]></dc:creator></item><item><title>Void screaming</title><link>https://imgur.com/gallery/vqwEQwS</link><description><![CDATA[<div><img alt="" src="https://i.imgur.com/MBvuMpM.png" style="max-width: 90%; height: auto;"></div><div><span></span></div><div>She and I have been in couples therapy for a while now, we both are in individual therapy as well. 

